WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.015 --> 00:00:05.839
In a laboratory in Japan, a scientist examines an ancient block of ice.

2
00:00:05.841 --> 00:00:13.374
It is hard to distinguish at first glance, but the scientist can make out a long, curved tusk encased within it.

3
00:00:13.375 --> 00:00:21.702
Inside this mass of ice is the frozen carcass of a baby woolly mammoth that was discovered in the tundra of Siberia.

4
00:00:21.703 --> 00:00:29.298
With great care the scientist slowly removes ice from the body, hoping to find intact DNA.

5
00:00:29.299 --> 00:00:32.854
The woolly mammoth is an extinct relative of the elephant.

6
00:00:32.855 --> 00:00:40.849
Even though a common ancestor lived many, many thousands of years ago, the elephant is not a direct descendant of the mammoth.

7
00:00:40.850 --> 00:00:48.117
In fact, the species diverged into separate evolutionary branches as long as six million years ago.

8
00:00:48.118 --> 00:00:53.530
The woolly mammoth first appeared only two million years ago, during the Pleistocene epoch.

9
00:00:53.531 --> 00:00:59.649
They survived until as recently as ten thousand years ago, when the last ice age ended.

10
00:00:59.650 --> 00:01:04.507
You could say that elephants are evolutionary cousins of the woolly mammoth.

11
00:01:04.508 --> 00:01:10.040
The most obvious difference between these distant cousins is, of course, hair.

12
00:01:10.041 --> 00:01:16.477
Shaggy fur, which could grow up to fifty centimeters long, kept the woolly mammoth warm.

13
00:01:16.478 --> 00:01:22.140
Elephants have hair as well, but it is short and not thick enough to cover their hides.

14
00:01:22.141 --> 00:01:26.656
The prehistoric woolly mammoth grew larger than today's elephants.

15
00:01:26.657 --> 00:01:36.186
While archaeological evidence shows that mammoths grew up to four and a half meters high, the largest African elephants can only reach four meters.

16
00:01:36.187 --> 00:01:40.345
Like their elephant cousins, woolly mammoths had tusks.

17
00:01:40.346 --> 00:01:46.357
When two elephants in a herd challenge each other, they meet head to head and lock tusks.

18
00:01:46.358 --> 00:01:49.412
Mammoths probably wrestled in the same way.

19
00:01:49.413 --> 00:01:57.869
But the woolly mammoths' tusks were thicker than elephant tusks, and almost twice as long; some reached five meters in length.

20
00:01:57.870 --> 00:02:06.310
Another difference is that mammoth tusks grew in long, sweeping arcs that could reach the ground when the mammoth lowered its head.

21
00:02:06.311 --> 00:02:13.830
Mammoths probably lived in herds, as their elephant cousins do today. But the mammoth lived during an ice age.

22
00:02:13.831 --> 00:02:18.597
How did such a large animal survive in such an inhospitable environment?

23
00:02:18.598 --> 00:02:23.390
Woolly mammoths had more than a thick coat to protect them from the harsh weather.

24
00:02:23.391 --> 00:02:31.658
Underneath their hides woolly mammoths had a layer of fat as thick as eight centimeters, which insulated them from the cold.

25
00:02:31.659 --> 00:02:38.269
The woolly mammoth's cold environment also explains why elephants and mammoths have such different ears.

26
00:02:38.270 --> 00:02:44.257
African and Indian elephants have large ears, sometimes two square meters in size.

27
00:02:44.258 --> 00:02:51.025
During the hot summer, an African elephant fans its ears, sending a cool breeze over its body.

28
00:02:51.026 --> 00:03:00.463
Ears are full of tiny blood vessels close to the skin's surface, and waving its large, thin ears also helps an elephant release heat from its body.

29
00:03:00.464 --> 00:03:04.883
Of course, mammoths had to face cold weather, not hot.

30
00:03:04.884 --> 00:03:12.732
Their ears evolved into a much smaller size so that their bodies would retain heat and not lose it to the cold air.

31
00:03:12.733 --> 00:03:20.621
Woolly mammoths were herbivores, and finding food in the snow-covered plains of the last ice age was quite difficult.

32
00:03:20.622 --> 00:03:28.000
But even today large herbivores, like the reindeer and the yak, live in great numbers in northern climates.

33
00:03:28.001 --> 00:03:31.111
Vegetation can be found under the snow.

34
00:03:31.112 --> 00:03:40.871
Many scientists believe that mammoths made good use of their tusks, swinging them from side to side to clear away snow and expose the plants underneath.

35
00:03:40.872 --> 00:03:45.542
The mammoth's long, curved tusks seem suited to this purpose.

36
00:03:45.543 --> 00:03:54.750
Many types of nutritious plant life that are extinct today grew during the last ice age, so the woolly mammoth would have had enough to feed on.

37
00:03:54.751 --> 00:04:03.449
Also mammoth herds likely traveled across the vast North to find the best feeding areas, as reindeer do today.

38
00:04:03.450 --> 00:04:08.242
The woolly mammoth's environment was very different from the one we know today.

39
00:04:08.243 --> 00:04:17.443
During the Pleistocene, giant sloths as tall as elephants, relatives of today's slow-moving tree sloths, walked the earth.

40
00:04:17.444 --> 00:04:23.553
The strange glyptodon, with its turtle-like shell, was as big as a car.

41
00:04:23.554 --> 00:04:28.435
Saber-toothed tigers and giant bears were dangerous predators.

42
00:04:28.436 --> 00:04:34.374
As the last ice age came to an end, most of these large animals became extinct.

43
00:04:34.375 --> 00:04:42.581
The giant sloth, woolly rhinoceros, cave lion, saber-toothed tiger, and many others all died out.

44
00:04:42.582 --> 00:04:45.502
This was also the end of the woolly mammoth.

45
00:04:45.503 --> 00:04:53.481
For many years scientists suspected that humankind's ancestors were responsible for hunting these animals to extinction.

46
00:04:53.482 --> 00:04:58.481
But scientists now believe that climate change was also partly responsible.

47
00:04:58.482 --> 00:05:05.915
The warming temperatures at the end of the last ice age led to a change in the species of plants available for food.

48
00:05:05.916 --> 00:05:13.343
The combination of rising temperatures and different plants was too difficult for the giant herbivores to adapt to.

49
00:05:13.344 --> 00:05:17.073
There was one notable and strange exception, however.

50
00:05:17.074 --> 00:05:26.684
Some woolly mammoths survived on the small and remote Wrangel Island in the Russian Arctic until approximately 1700 BC.

51
00:05:26.685 --> 00:05:35.073
The island was too small to support a large number of full-size mammoths, but a population of dwarf mammoths survived there.

52
00:05:35.074 --> 00:05:43.591
To this day Wrangel Island supports a greater number of Arctic plants than any other place on the planet of similar size.

53
00:05:43.592 --> 00:05:53.093
It's intriguing to think that in 2000 BC, when the great pyramids were being built in Egypt, woolly mammoths still wandered the plains.

54
00:05:53.094 --> 00:05:59.855
Most of what we know about mammoths comes from the study of frozen carcasses found in northern Siberia.

55
00:05:59.856 --> 00:06:06.965
Only a few dozen mammoth remains have been found, and bodies that are complete are very rare.

56
00:06:06.966 --> 00:06:19.462
But unlike the fossil and skeleton remains of other extinct species, the frozen remains of mammoths allow scientists to study their skin, flesh, and sometimes their internal organs.

57
00:06:19.463 --> 00:06:24.620
Some scientists are investigating ways to bring the woolly mammoth back to life.

58
00:06:24.621 --> 00:06:34.455
One proposal involves extracting frozen sperm from a preserved mammoth's reproductive organs, and using it to fertilize the egg of a female elephant.

59
00:06:34.456 --> 00:06:37.502
Another method would be to clone the animal.

60
00:06:37.503 --> 00:06:47.651
Scientists would have to extract DNA from a piece of a preserved mammoth carcass, then replace the DNA of an elephant's egg with the mammoth's DNA.

61
00:06:47.652 --> 00:06:56.151
Scientists would stimulate the egg to start growing and place it inside a female elephant for the duration of the gestation period.

62
00:06:56.152 --> 00:06:59.262
There are problems with both of these techniques.

63
00:06:59.263 --> 00:07:05.757
Even under laboratory conditions, it is hard for egg and sperm cells to survive freezing.

64
00:07:05.758 --> 00:07:15.750
Furthermore, if intact sperm cells from a woolly mammoth could be found, using them to fertilize an elephant egg would not bring the mammoth back to life.

65
00:07:15.751 --> 00:07:21.783
Instead it would create an animal that is a hybrid of the woolly mammoth and the elephant.

66
00:07:21.784 --> 00:07:24.561
Cloning is a more promising method.

67
00:07:24.562 --> 00:07:33.478
For many years the idea of cloning a mammoth seemed ridiculous because DNA from a frozen carcass is typically fragmented.

68
00:07:33.479 --> 00:07:43.336
But recently geneticists have made progress analyzing fragmented DNA, and a significant portion of mammoth DNA has been reassembled.

69
00:07:43.337 --> 00:07:54.079
It may take many years, but one day scientists may clone the long-dead cousin to the elephant, and the woolly mammoth will walk the earth again.

